// Unit 2 "Stocks", Chapter 4: Charts & Indicators (technical analysis).
export default {
  id: 'stocks-ch4',
  title: 'Charts & Indicators',
  blurb: 'Read candles, trends, levels and indicators like a pro, then learn exactly where chart reading stops working.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-candlesticks',
      title: 'Candlesticks: Four Prices, One Shape',
      summary: 'Every candle packs open, high, low and close into one little shape. Learn to read bodies, wicks and the famous patterns.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'candle',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'A whole fight in one shape', html: `<p>Open almost any trading app and the chart is made of little colored boxes with lines poking out. Those are <span class="term" data-def="A chart symbol that shows the open, high, low and close price for one time period.">candlesticks</span>, and each one is a tiny recap of a battle between buyers and sellers.</p><p>Every candle stores <strong>four prices</strong> for one slice of time:</p><ul><li><strong>Open</strong>: the first trade of the period</li><li><strong>High</strong>: the highest price anyone paid</li><li><strong>Low</strong>: the lowest price anyone accepted</li><li><strong>Close</strong>: the last trade of the period</li></ul><p>Traders shorten this to <span class="hl">OHLC</span>. A line chart only shows you the close. A candle shows the whole story: where the price started, how far it wandered, and where it ended up.</p><p>Tradition credits rice traders in 18th-century Japan with the idea. Western traders adopted it widely in the 1990s, and now it is the default view on basically every platform.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "Four numbers, one shape. Candles are the most information-dense doodle in finance. Hoo knew?" } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'candlestick-anatomy', caption: 'The thick part is the body (open to close). The thin lines are wicks, reaching up to the high and down to the low. Green means it closed higher than it opened; red means lower.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bodies and wicks', html: `<p>The <span class="term" data-def="The thick rectangle of a candle, spanning the open price and the close price.">body</span> is the rectangle between the open and the close. If the close is <em>above</em> the open, the candle is usually drawn green (or hollow). If the close is <em>below</em> the open, it is red (or filled).</p><p>The <span class="term" data-def="The thin line above or below a candle body showing how far price traveled beyond the open and close. Also called a shadow.">wicks</span> (also called shadows) show the extremes. The top of the upper wick is the high. The bottom of the lower wick is the low.</p><p>Here is how to read them like sentences:</p><ul><li><strong>Big body, tiny wicks</strong>: one side was in control the whole time.</li><li><strong>Long lower wick</strong>: sellers shoved price down, then buyers shoved it back up. Rejection of lower prices.</li><li><strong>Long upper wick</strong>: buyers tried to push higher and got slapped back. Rejection of higher prices.</li><li><strong>Tiny body, wicks on both sides</strong>: a tug of war nobody won.</li></ul><p>Color tells you <em>who won the period</em>. Wicks tell you <em>how hard the loser fought</em>.</p>` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'candle-builder', props: {}, caption: 'Drag the open, high, low and close. Make a big green candle, then a candle with a long lower wick, then one where open and close are almost equal. Watch how the shape tells the story.' },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A candle opens at $50, hits a high of $53, a low of $49, and closes at $52. What does it look like?', options: ['Green body from $50 to $52, upper wick to $53, lower wick to $49', 'Red body from $52 to $50, wicks to $53 and $49', 'Green body from $49 to $53 with no wicks', 'Red body from $53 to $49'], answer: 0, explain: 'Close ($52) is above open ($50), so the body is green from 50 to 52. The wicks stretch to the high ($53) and the low ($49).' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'One candle, many timeframes', html: `<p>A candle does not have a fixed length of time. You pick the <span class="term" data-def="How much time each candle on a chart represents, such as 1 minute, 1 day or 1 week.">timeframe</span>. On a <strong>1-minute chart</strong>, each candle is one minute of trading. On a <strong>daily chart</strong>, each candle is a full trading day. Weekly and monthly charts zoom out even more.</p><p>Here is the wild part: the same stretch of trading can look totally different depending on the zoom. A daily candle with a long lower wick might be made of 390 one-minute candles (a regular session runs 9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m. ET) that show a scary morning crash followed by a steady afternoon recovery.</p><p>Leo checks the daily chart of a stock once a week and sees a calm uptrend. Dev stares at the 1-minute chart all day and sees chaos. Same stock. Different lens.</p><p>Rule of thumb: <span class="hl">bigger timeframes carry more weight</span>. A pattern on a weekly chart reflects millions of shares and many days of decisions. A pattern on a 1-minute chart might be one big order.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Shorter timeframes mean more signals and more noise. More trades is not the same as more money. Ask Dev." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A red candle on a daily chart always means the stock was down for the day compared with the previous close.', answer: false, explain: 'A red candle means the close was below that candle\'s own open. If the stock gapped up at the open and then faded a little, it can close red while still being up versus yesterday\'s close.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The single-candle classics', html: `<p>A few shapes have names because traders watch them so often:</p><ul><li><strong>Doji</strong>: open and close are nearly equal, so the body is a thin line. It signals indecision. After a long run up, a doji says buyers are losing their grip.</li><li><strong>Hammer</strong>: small body near the top, long lower wick (often at least twice the body), little or no upper wick. It shows up <em>after a decline</em> and says sellers pushed hard but buyers won the close.</li><li><strong>Shooting star</strong>: the upside-down hammer. Small body near the bottom, long upper wick, appearing <em>after a rise</em>. Buyers pushed up and got rejected.</li></ul><p>Context is everything. The same hammer shape that appears <em>after a rally</em> gets a different name, the <span class="term" data-def="A candle shaped like a hammer that appears after an uptrend, read as a possible warning that selling pressure is building.">hanging man</span>, and is read as a warning instead of a hopeful sign. Shape plus location equals meaning.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two-candle combos: engulfing', html: `<p>An <span class="term" data-def="A two-candle pattern where the second candle's body completely covers the first candle's body and is the opposite color.">engulfing pattern</span> happens when the second candle's body completely swallows the first candle's body.</p><ul><li><strong>Bullish engulfing</strong>: a small red candle, then a bigger green candle whose body covers it top to bottom. Buyers took over. Most meaningful after a decline.</li><li><strong>Bearish engulfing</strong>: a small green candle, then a bigger red candle that covers it. Sellers took over. Most meaningful after a rise.</li></ul><p>Maya watches a stock drift down for two weeks. Then one day it opens below the prior close and rallies hard to close above the previous day's open. That is a bullish engulfing candle. Does it guarantee a bottom? No. It tells her the balance of power shifted <em>for one day</em>. She would want to see follow-through the next day, ideally with strong volume, before trusting it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Patterns are clues, not promises. A hammer says 'buyers showed up.' It does not say 'buyers will keep showing up.'" } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Candle cheat sheet', cards: [
          { front: 'Doji', back: 'Open and close nearly equal. Indecision; most interesting after a long move.' },
          { front: 'Hammer', back: 'Small body on top, long lower wick, after a decline. Buyers rejected lower prices.' },
          { front: 'Shooting star', back: 'Small body on the bottom, long upper wick, after a rise. Sellers rejected higher prices.' },
          { front: 'Hanging man', back: 'Hammer shape that appears after a rise. Read as a warning, not a buy signal.' },
          { front: 'Bullish engulfing', back: 'Big green body fully covers the prior red body. Buyers seized control for the day.' },
          { front: 'Bearish engulfing', back: 'Big red body fully covers the prior green body. Sellers seized control for the day.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each candle to what it suggests.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Doji', right: 'Indecision between buyers and sellers' },
          { left: 'Hammer after a drop', right: 'Buyers rejected lower prices' },
          { left: 'Shooting star after a rally', right: 'Sellers rejected higher prices' },
          { left: 'Bearish engulfing', right: 'Sellers overpowered the prior day completely' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: one candle can call the top', html: `<p>Single candles fail all the time. Researchers who have tested candle patterns have generally found that, on their own, many show weak or inconsistent predictive power once trading costs are included. Treat them as <span class="hl">a reason to pay attention</span>, then look for confirmation: the next candle, volume, and where the pattern sits relative to support, resistance and the trend.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What four prices does a candlestick show?', options: ['Bid, ask, last, volume', 'Open, high, low, close', 'Open, close, average, volume', 'High, low, median, close'], answer: 1, explain: 'OHLC: open, high, low, close for one time period.' },
          { q: 'A candle has a tiny body near the top and a long lower wick, appearing after a two-week decline. What is it called?', options: ['Shooting star', 'Doji', 'Hammer', 'Bearish engulfing'], answer: 2, explain: 'A hammer: buyers rejected the lows. If the same shape appeared after a rally, it would be a hanging man.' },
          { q: 'On a 5-minute chart, what does one candle represent?', options: ['Five minutes of trading', 'Five days of trading', 'Five trades', 'Five percent of daily volume'], answer: 0, explain: 'The timeframe sets how much time each candle covers.' },
          { q: 'Which description fits a bearish engulfing pattern?', options: ['Two red candles of equal size', 'A doji followed by a hammer', 'A small red candle followed by a big green one that covers it', 'A small green candle followed by a big red one whose body covers it'], answer: 3, explain: 'Bearish engulfing: the second, red body swallows the first, green body. Sellers overpowered the prior session.' },
          { q: 'Dev sees a hammer on a 1-minute chart and buys a big position instantly. What is the biggest flaw?', options: ['Hammers only work on weekly charts', 'He acted on a single short-timeframe candle with no confirmation', 'Hammers are always bearish', 'He should have waited for a doji'], answer: 1, explain: 'One small-timeframe candle is weak evidence. Confirmation (next candle, volume, location near support) and a planned stop matter far more.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-trends',
      title: 'Trends: Stairs Up, Stairs Down',
      summary: 'Higher highs, lower lows, and the sideways chop in between. Learn to label market structure on any timeframe.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Prices move in stairs, not ramps', html: `<p>Zoom out on almost any stock chart and you will notice something: price almost never moves in a straight line. It climbs, pulls back, climbs again. Or it drops, bounces, drops again. Those zigzags have a name: <span class="term" data-def="The pattern of swing highs and swing lows that shows whether price is trending up, trending down or moving sideways.">market structure</span>.</p><p>Each peak in the zigzag is a <span class="term" data-def="A local peak on a chart: a high with lower highs on both sides of it.">swing high</span>. Each valley is a <span class="term" data-def="A local valley on a chart: a low with higher lows on both sides of it.">swing low</span>. Once you can spot those, you can describe any chart in one of three words:</p><ul><li><strong>Uptrend</strong></li><li><strong>Downtrend</strong></li><li><strong>Range</strong> (sideways)</li></ul><p>This sounds basic. It is. It is also the single most useful skill in chart reading, because almost every indicator and pattern you will learn later means something different depending on which of these three states the market is in.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Before you ask 'buy or sell?', ask 'up, down, or sideways?' Most bad trades skip that question." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'trends', caption: 'An uptrend makes higher highs and higher lows. A downtrend makes lower highs and lower lows. A range bounces between roughly the same high and low.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The uptrend recipe: HH and HL', html: `<p>An <span class="hl">uptrend</span> is a series of <strong>higher highs (HH)</strong> and <strong>higher lows (HL)</strong>. Each rally pushes past the last peak, and each pullback stops above the last valley.</p><p>Say a stock does this over a few weeks:</p><ul><li>Rallies to $40, pulls back to $36</li><li>Rallies to $44, pulls back to $39</li><li>Rallies to $47, pulls back to $42</li></ul><p>Highs: 40, 44, 47 (higher). Lows: 36, 39, 42 (higher). Textbook uptrend. Buyers are so eager that they step in at higher and higher prices on each dip.</p><p>The higher lows matter most. As long as pullbacks keep holding above the previous swing low, the trend is intact. The moment price falls <em>below</em> the most recent higher low, the structure is broken. That does not automatically mean a downtrend has started, but the clean staircase is over.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Swing highs: $20, $23, $22. Swing lows: $17, $19, $18. What is the best description?', options: ['Clean uptrend: higher highs and higher lows', 'Clean downtrend: lower highs and lower lows', 'The uptrend has stalled: the last high and last low both came in lower', 'Not enough information to say anything'], answer: 2, explain: 'The first two swings were an uptrend (20 to 23, 17 to 19). Then the high slipped to 22 and the low to 18. The staircase broke; that is a warning sign, though not yet a full downtrend.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The downtrend recipe: LH and LL', html: `<p>Flip it over. A <span class="hl">downtrend</span> is a series of <strong>lower highs (LH)</strong> and <strong>lower lows (LL)</strong>. Each bounce fails below the last peak. Each drop breaks under the last valley.</p><p>In a downtrend, the bounces are the trap. A stock falls from $80 to $60, rebounds to $70, and people on social media shout "bottom is in!" Then it drops to $55. Then it bounces to $64. Then $50. Each bounce looked like hope. The structure said otherwise the whole time: lower highs, lower lows.</p><p>There is an old saying: <em>"Don't try to catch a falling knife."</em> Buying a downtrending stock just because it looks "cheap" compared with where it used to be is one of the most common beginner mistakes. A stock that fell 50% can fall another 50%.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Down 50% then down 50% again is down 75% total. 'It can't go lower' is not a strategy." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Ranges: the sideways grind', html: `<p>Lots of the time, stocks do neither. They bounce between a ceiling and a floor, making roughly <strong>equal highs and equal lows</strong>. That is a <span class="term" data-def="A period where price moves sideways between a fairly steady high and low, with no clear trend.">range</span>, also called consolidation or chop.</p><p>Ranges happen when buyers and sellers roughly agree on value for a while. Maybe the company is between earnings reports and there is no fresh news. Maybe a big fund is quietly buying while another quietly sells.</p><p>Ranges matter because strategies that work beautifully in trends get chopped to pieces in ranges. A trend-following tool keeps saying "breakout!" and then price snaps back inside. Bolt the bot's EMA Crossover strategy, for example, tends to struggle in choppy sideways markets because the averages keep crossing back and forth.</p><p>Eventually, ranges end with a <strong>breakout</strong> (up) or <strong>breakdown</strong> (down). Next lesson covers how to tell real breaks from fake ones.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'A stock is trending up. Put the swing points in the order they happened.', items: ['Swing low at $30', 'Swing high at $35', 'Swing low at $32', 'Swing high at $38', 'Swing low at $34'], explain: 'An uptrend alternates highs and lows, each higher than the last of its kind: lows 30, 32, 34 and highs 35, 38.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Timeframes can disagree', html: `<p>Here is where it gets interesting. A stock can be in a <strong>daily uptrend</strong> and a <strong>15-minute downtrend</strong> at the same time. That is not a contradiction. The 15-minute downtrend might just be one pullback inside the bigger daily staircase.</p><p>Many traders use a <span class="hl">top-down</span> approach:</p><ol><li>Check the weekly or daily chart for the main trend.</li><li>Drop to a smaller timeframe to time an entry.</li><li>Prefer trades that go <em>with</em> the bigger trend.</li></ol><p>Ms. Ortiz thinks about this like weather and climate. Today might be cold and rainy (the short timeframe), but that does not mean summer is cancelled (the long timeframe). She would not sell her ice cream machine because of one chilly afternoon in July.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Fractal fact: zigzags contain smaller zigzags. Every daily swing is built from many intraday swings." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How trends end (and trendlines)', html: `<p>Trends do not last forever, and they rarely end with a neon sign. Usually the warning signs pile up one at a time:</p><ul><li><strong>Momentum fades.</strong> Each new high is only a little higher than the last, and the rallies get shorter.</li><li><strong>Pullbacks get deeper.</strong> Dips that used to stop after 3% now run 8%.</li><li><strong>A higher low breaks.</strong> This is the big one. The staircase cracks.</li><li><strong>A lower high forms.</strong> The next bounce fails below the prior peak. Now you have the first piece of a possible downtrend.</li></ul><p>Some traders also draw a <span class="term" data-def="A sloped line connecting two or more swing lows in an uptrend (or swing highs in a downtrend).">trendline</span> connecting the swing lows of an uptrend. A clean break of that line is another early warning. Trendlines are handy, but they are subjective: shift your starting point by one candle and the line changes. Swing highs and lows are harder to argue with.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Draw it before you trade it', html: `<p>Before placing any practice trade on My Desk, open the chart and label the last three swing highs and lows. Write down "uptrend," "downtrend," or "range" in your journal. If you cannot decide, that itself is an answer: <span class="hl">unclear structure usually means stand aside</span>. Sitting out is a position too, and it costs nothing.</p><p>Then check one timeframe higher. If the daily says uptrend and your entry chart says downtrend, you know you are buying a pullback, not a fresh rally, and you can plan around that.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A stock that has fallen 60% from its high is safer to buy than one that has fallen 10%, because it has less room to drop.', answer: false, explain: 'Price falling does not make something safer. A stock down 60% can still fall a lot further (it could still go to zero). Look at structure and fundamentals, not just distance from the old high.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Structure vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Higher high (HH)', back: 'A swing peak above the previous swing peak.' },
          { front: 'Higher low (HL)', back: 'A pullback that stops above the previous swing low. The backbone of an uptrend.' },
          { front: 'Lower high (LH)', back: 'A bounce that fails below the previous peak.' },
          { front: 'Lower low (LL)', back: 'A drop that breaks below the previous valley.' },
          { front: 'Range', back: 'Sideways movement between a steady ceiling and floor.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which combination defines an uptrend?', options: ['Lower highs and higher lows', 'Higher highs and higher lows', 'Higher highs and lower lows', 'Equal highs and equal lows'], answer: 1, explain: 'Uptrend = HH + HL. Each peak and each valley is higher than the one before.' },
          { q: 'In a clean uptrend, which event most clearly breaks the structure?', options: ['A new higher high', 'A small red candle', 'Price closing below the most recent higher low', 'Volume dropping on a pullback'], answer: 2, explain: 'Higher lows are the backbone. Losing the last one breaks the staircase.' },
          { q: 'A stock has bounced between $48 and $52 for six weeks. What is it in?', options: ['A downtrend', 'An uptrend', 'A breakout', 'A range'], answer: 3, explain: 'Roughly equal highs and lows = a range (consolidation).' },
          { q: 'Leo sees a daily uptrend but a 15-minute downtrend. What is the most sensible reading?', options: ['The short-term drop may just be a pullback inside the larger uptrend', 'The charts are broken', 'The daily trend has definitely ended', 'He should only ever look at the 1-minute chart'], answer: 0, explain: 'Timeframes nest. A lower-timeframe downtrend is often a pullback within a higher-timeframe uptrend.' },
          { q: 'Why do trend-following strategies often struggle in ranges?', options: ['Ranges have no volume', 'Brokers block trend trades in ranges', 'Signals keep firing and then reversing, causing repeated small losses', 'Ranges only happen on weekends'], answer: 2, explain: 'In chop, every "breakout" signal tends to snap back, which bleeds a trend strategy with small losses and costs.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-support-resistance',
      title: 'Support & Resistance: Floors and Ceilings',
      summary: 'Why prices keep bouncing off the same levels, why old ceilings become new floors, and how to tell a real breakout from a fakeout.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Prices have memory', html: `<p>Stocks have an odd habit: they keep stopping at the same prices. A stock bounces off $50 in March, again in May, again in August. Another keeps stalling at $75 like it hit an invisible ceiling.</p><p>Those levels are <span class="term" data-def="A price area where buying has repeatedly been strong enough to stop a decline.">support</span> (a floor where buyers keep showing up) and <span class="term" data-def="A price area where selling has repeatedly been strong enough to stop a rise.">resistance</span> (a ceiling where sellers keep showing up).</p><p>This is not magic. It is people. Markets are crowds of humans and algorithms making decisions, and crowds remember prices. If a stock bounced hard from $50 last time, plenty of traders will be watching $50 again, and some will place buy orders there. Those orders help make the bounce happen again.</p><p>Support and resistance are <span class="hl">zones, not laser lines</span>. Think "$49.50 to $50.50," not "exactly $50.00."</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Floors and ceilings. If you can draw two horizontal lines, you can do this." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'support-resistance', caption: 'Price bounces between a support zone below and a resistance zone above. Notice the touches cluster in areas, not single exact prices.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why levels form', html: `<p>Three forces build support and resistance:</p><ul><li><strong>Regret and memory.</strong> Maya almost bought at $50, hesitated, and watched it run to $60. If it ever comes back to $50, she is buying. Thousands of Mayas form support.</li><li><strong>Breakeven sellers.</strong> Leo bought at $75 right before a drop to $60. He has been staring at red for months. If it gets back to $75, he just wants out. Thousands of Leos form resistance.</li><li><strong>Resting orders.</strong> Big investors often place limit orders at specific prices. Round numbers ($100, $50) and prior highs and lows attract piles of these orders, which you can sometimes see stacking up in the order book.</li></ul><p>The more times a level has been tested and held, and the more volume that traded there, the more people notice it. That is why traders say a level with <span class="hl">three clean touches</span> is more significant than one with a single bounce. But careful: each test can also chew through the waiting orders, so a level can weaken the more it gets hit.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Why might a stock struggle to rise above a price where it dropped sharply months earlier?', options: ['Exchanges block trades above old highs', 'Trapped buyers from that level may sell to get back to breakeven', 'Stocks cannot rise above a previous high', 'Resistance only exists on weekly charts'], answer: 1, explain: 'People who bought near the old high and sat through a loss often sell when price returns there, creating supply (resistance).' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Role reversal: ceilings become floors', html: `<p>Here is one of the most useful ideas in chart reading. When price finally breaks <em>through</em> resistance, that old ceiling often becomes the new floor. This is called <span class="term" data-def="When a broken resistance level later acts as support, or a broken support level later acts as resistance.">role reversal</span> or "polarity."</p><p>Why? Picture a stock stuck under $75 for months. It finally breaks out to $80. Now:</p><ul><li>Traders who missed the breakout want a second chance and will buy a dip toward $75.</li><li>Traders who shorted below $75 are losing money and may buy to cover if price comes back there.</li><li>The sellers who used to defend $75 are mostly gone, since their supply got absorbed by the breakout.</li></ul><p>So a pullback to $75 often bounces. Traders call it a <span class="hl">retest</span>. It works in reverse too: broken support often becomes resistance. When a stock falls through a $40 floor, rallies back to $40 frequently get sold.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "Old ceiling, new floor. It is like the upstairs neighbor's floor being your ceiling. Same slab, different side." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'After a stock breaks above resistance at $75, a later pullback to around $75 that holds is often called a retest.', answer: true, explain: 'A successful retest is when the old resistance acts as support on the pullback. Many breakout traders wait for this before entering.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Breakouts vs fakeouts', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="When price moves decisively through a support or resistance level.">breakout</span> is when price pushes through a level. A <span class="term" data-def="A move through a level that quickly reverses back inside, trapping traders who acted on the breakout. Also called a false breakout.">fakeout</span> is when it pokes through and then snaps right back, trapping everyone who jumped in.</p><p>Fakeouts happen constantly. Lots of traders place stop-loss orders just beyond obvious levels, and price sometimes runs those stops before reversing. Some clues that a breakout is more likely real:</p><ul><li><strong>Volume surge.</strong> A breakout on heavy volume (well above average) shows real demand. A breakout on thin volume is suspicious.</li><li><strong>Close beyond the level.</strong> A wick through resistance that closes back below it is a rejection, not a breakout. Many traders wait for a candle to <em>close</em> beyond the level.</li><li><strong>Follow-through.</strong> Does the next candle or next day keep going?</li><li><strong>Context.</strong> Breakouts in the direction of the larger trend tend to be more reliable than breakouts against it.</li></ul><p>None of these guarantee anything. They tilt the odds.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: "Dev's fakeout", html: `<p>A stock had stalled at $30 three times. At 10:05 a.m. it ticked to $30.20 and Dev bought 500 shares with no stop. Volume was light. By 10:20 it was back at $29.40, and by lunch it was $28.70. He was down $750 and still "waiting for it to come back."</p><p>What would have helped: waiting for a candle to close above $30, checking that volume was heavy, and setting a stop just below the level <em>before</em> buying.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'Where the stops hide', html: `<p>Because so many traders set stop-loss orders just below obvious support or just above obvious resistance, those spots can hold a cluster of orders waiting to trigger. When price touches them, the stops turn into market orders and can briefly accelerate the move, which is one reason quick "stop runs" through a level and back are so common. This is also why many traders place stops a bit beyond the zone instead of exactly on the line, sized so the loss is still small if it hits.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each situation to the best description.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Price bounces off $50 for the third time', right: 'Support holding' },
          { left: 'Price pokes above $80 then closes back below', right: 'Fakeout or rejection' },
          { left: 'Old $75 ceiling now stops a pullback', right: 'Role reversal' },
          { left: 'Price closes above resistance on triple the usual volume', right: 'Breakout with confirmation' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Drawing levels without fooling yourself', html: `<p>Give ten people a chart and ask them to draw support and resistance, and you will get a dozen different answers. A few habits keep you honest:</p><ul><li><strong>Start on the daily chart.</strong> Levels visible there matter to more people than levels on a 2-minute chart.</li><li><strong>Look for obvious turning points.</strong> If you have to squint, it is not a level most people will act on.</li><li><strong>Use zones.</strong> Draw a band, not a hairline.</li><li><strong>Fewer is better.</strong> A chart with fifteen lines on it will always have a line near the current price, which "proves" whatever you already wanted to believe.</li></ul><p>Support and resistance are also where many traders put their stops and targets. A long trade might use support as the stop area and the next resistance as the target. You will do exactly that in the risk lessons in the next chapter.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Support is where buyers showed up before. Nothing forces them to show up again. Always know your exit if the floor gives way." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya buys at $52 with support at $50 and resistance at $58. If she exits at resistance, how many dollars per share does she gain?', answer: 6, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '$58 - $52 = $6 per share of potential reward. Her risk if support fails (stop just under $50) would be a bit over $2 per share.' },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is support?', options: ['A price area where buying has repeatedly stopped declines', 'The highest price of the year', 'A government program that props up stock prices', 'The average of the last 20 closes'], answer: 0, explain: 'Support is a floor created by repeated buying interest at a price area.' },
          { q: 'Why do round numbers like $100 often act as support or resistance?', options: ['Exchanges require it', 'Many traders place orders and set alerts at round numbers', 'Prices cannot trade at round numbers', 'It is a coincidence with no explanation'], answer: 1, explain: 'People anchor on round numbers and cluster orders there, which builds up supply or demand.' },
          { q: 'A stock breaks below support at $40, then rallies back to $40 and stalls. What is happening?', options: ['A golden cross', 'A hammer', 'A bullish breakout', 'Broken support acting as resistance'], answer: 3, explain: 'Role reversal works both ways. Old floors often become new ceilings.' },
          { q: 'Which is the strongest clue that a breakout is real?', options: ['It happens on light volume during lunch', 'A wick pokes above resistance but the candle closes below', 'Price closes above resistance on heavy volume and follows through', 'Someone on social media says it is real'], answer: 2, explain: 'A close beyond the level, strong volume and follow-through are the classic confirmations.' },
          { q: 'Why is drawing fifteen support and resistance lines on one chart a problem?', options: ['There will always be a line near price, so you can justify any trade', 'Charting software limits you to five lines', 'More lines always make predictions more accurate', 'Lines expire after one day'], answer: 0, explain: 'Too many lines turn analysis into confirmation bias. Focus on the obvious, widely watched levels.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-volume',
      title: 'Volume: The Lie Detector',
      summary: 'Price tells you where the stock went. Volume tells you how many people actually cared. Learn confirmation, climaxes and relative volume.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The bars under the chart', html: `<p>Under most price charts sits a row of vertical bars. That is <span class="term" data-def="The number of shares traded during a period of time.">volume</span>: how many shares changed hands during each candle. If a stock traded 4 million shares today, the daily volume bar is 4 million tall.</p><p>Every share traded has a buyer <em>and</em> a seller, so volume does not tell you which side "won." Price direction tells you that. What volume tells you is <span class="hl">how much conviction was behind the move</span>.</p><p>Think of it like a crowd at a concert. A song that gets polite clapping from twelve people is different from one that makes an entire stadium jump. Same song, very different energy. A stock rising 3% on light volume is the twelve people. A stock rising 3% on four times its normal volume is the stadium.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Price is what happened. Volume is how loudly it happened." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'volume', caption: 'Volume bars sit under the price chart. Notice the tall bars cluster at breakouts and big turning points, while quiet drifts come with short bars.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Volume confirms (or doubts) the trend', html: `<p>A classic rule of thumb from the earliest days of chart reading: <strong>volume should go with the trend</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>Healthy uptrend:</strong> volume tends to swell on up days and shrink on pullbacks. Buyers are eager; sellers are half-hearted.</li><li><strong>Healthy downtrend:</strong> volume swells on down days and shrinks on bounces.</li><li><strong>Warning sign:</strong> price grinds to new highs, but each push comes on <em>less</em> volume. Fewer people are willing to pay up. The rally may be running on fumes.</li></ul><p>None of this is a law of physics. It is a way to ask a better question. When Leo sees a stock make a new high, he now asks, "Was anyone actually there?" If the new high came on half the normal volume, he is more skeptical than if it came on double.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'The four price-volume combos', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Price</th><th>Volume</th><th>Common reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up</td><td>Rising</td><td>Buyers are aggressive. Strong.</td></tr><tr><td>Up</td><td>Falling</td><td>Rally may be tiring. Watch closely.</td></tr><tr><td>Down</td><td>Rising</td><td>Sellers are aggressive. Weak.</td></tr><tr><td>Down</td><td>Falling</td><td>Selling may be drying up. Often a pause.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are tendencies, not rules. Use them to frame questions, then look at the trend, the levels and the news before deciding anything.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A stock in an uptrend pulls back 4% over three days on volume about half its normal level. What does this most likely suggest?', options: ['Heavy selling: the trend is over', 'Sellers lack conviction: the pullback may be a normal pause', 'Volume does not matter on pullbacks', 'The stock will be delisted'], answer: 1, explain: 'Light-volume pullbacks inside an uptrend suggest few people are eager to sell. That is often read as a healthy pause, though nothing is guaranteed.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Relative volume: compared to what?', html: `<p>Raw volume numbers mean little on their own. Two million shares is enormous for a small company and a sleepy morning for a mega-cap that trades tens of millions a day.</p><p>That is why traders use <span class="term" data-def="Current volume divided by the stock's normal (average) volume for the same period. 2.0 means twice the usual activity.">relative volume</span> (often written RVOL). It compares today's volume to the stock's normal volume:</p><p><strong>RVOL = current volume / average volume</strong></p><p>If a stock usually trades 1 million shares a day and today it trades 3 million, RVOL is 3. Something is going on: news, earnings, a big fund buying, an index change.</p><p>Intraday traders get fancier and compare volume <em>so far today</em> against the average volume <em>by this time of day</em>, because volume is not spread evenly. In US stocks it tends to be heaviest near the open and the close, with a lull around lunch. Strategies like Gap and Go and Opening Range Breakout lean heavily on high relative volume in the first part of the session.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: "My Gap and Go rules skip stocks with low relative volume. No crowd, no follow-through. Beep." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A stock averages 800,000 shares a day. Today it trades 2,000,000. What is its relative volume?', answer: 2.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '', explain: '2,000,000 / 800,000 = 2.5. Today is two and a half times as busy as normal.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Volume on breakouts', html: `<p>Last lesson you learned that breakouts often fail. Volume is one of the best filters.</p><p>When price pushes through resistance, the sellers who defended that level have to be overwhelmed. That takes real buying, which shows up as <span class="hl">a volume spike well above average</span>. Many breakout traders look for volume at least 1.5 to 2 times normal on the breakout candle or day, though there is no magic number.</p><p>A breakout on below-average volume is like a door that swung open in the wind. Maybe someone walked through. Maybe not. These thin breakouts are more prone to snapping back inside the range.</p><p>The CANSLIM approach, made famous by investor William O'Neil, specifically looks for stocks breaking out of a base on heavy volume. You can watch Bolt run a CANSLIM-style screen on My Desk and see how many candidates get rejected just for weak breakout volume.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Climaxes: when everyone shows up at once', html: `<p>Sometimes volume goes absolutely vertical: five, ten, even twenty times normal. At the end of a long move, that can signal a <span class="term" data-def="An extreme burst of volume at the end of a long move, often marking exhaustion when the last eager buyers or panicked sellers finally act.">climax</span>.</p><ul><li><strong>Selling climax:</strong> after a long decline, a day of panic selling on huge volume, often with a long lower wick. The last scared holders finally dump everything. With no one left to sell, price can bounce. Traders sometimes call this <em>capitulation</em>.</li><li><strong>Buying climax:</strong> after a long run-up, a frantic day of buying on huge volume, often with a big gap and a long upper wick. The last eager buyers pile in. With no one left to buy, the top may be near.</li></ul><p>Climaxes are easier to label in hindsight than in real time. A huge-volume down day can be capitulation, or it can be the first day of a much bigger collapse after terrible news.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Trying to catch the exact climax bottom is how people catch falling knives. Wait for proof the selling actually stopped." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each volume pattern to its usual reading.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Breakout on 3x average volume', right: 'Strong demand behind the move' },
          { left: 'New highs on shrinking volume', right: 'Rally may be losing steam' },
          { left: 'Huge volume panic day after a long drop', right: 'Possible selling climax' },
          { left: 'Pullback on light volume in an uptrend', right: 'Sellers lack conviction' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Volume can be misleading', html: `<p>Some volume spikes have nothing to do with conviction. Options expiration days, index rebalancing days, and the end of each quarter can produce big volume as funds adjust holdings mechanically. Volume also drops around holidays. Before reading a spike as a signal, check the calendar and the news.</p><p>Also remember that a lot of trading now happens off the main exchanges, in venues like dark pools and through wholesalers, and some charting apps show different volume totals depending on their data source. Compare a stock's volume to its own history on the same platform, not to a number you saw somewhere else.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'High volume on an up day proves that more people were buying than selling.', answer: false, explain: 'Every trade has a buyer and a seller, so share counts on both sides are always equal. High volume on an up day shows buyers were more aggressive (willing to pay up), not that there were more of them.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Volume vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Volume', back: 'Shares traded during a candle or a day.' },
          { front: 'Relative volume (RVOL)', back: 'Current volume compared with normal. Above 1 means busier than usual.' },
          { front: 'Confirmation', back: 'Volume rising in the direction of the price move, suggesting conviction.' },
          { front: 'Climax', back: 'Extreme volume at the end of a long move, often marking exhaustion.' },
          { front: 'Capitulation', back: 'Panicked mass selling that can mark the end of a decline.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What does a volume bar measure?', options: ['The dollar profit of buyers', 'The number of shares traded in that period', 'The number of buyers minus sellers', 'The percent price change'], answer: 1, explain: 'Volume counts shares traded. Every share has a buyer and a seller.' },
          { q: 'A stock averages 5 million shares a day. By noon it has traded 9 million. What can you say?', options: ['Relative volume is very low', 'Nothing; volume is meaningless', 'It is trading far more than usual, so something may be driving interest', 'It will definitely close higher'], answer: 2, explain: 'Already nearly double a full normal day by noon means heavy relative volume. It signals interest, not direction.' },
          { q: 'Which pattern most suggests a rally is weakening?', options: ['New highs on steadily shrinking volume', 'New highs on rising volume', 'A light-volume pullback', 'A breakout on 3x volume'], answer: 0, explain: 'When each new high attracts fewer shares, fewer buyers are willing to pay up.' },
          { q: 'Dev buys a breakout above resistance on volume 40% below average. What is the concern?', options: ['Low volume breakouts are illegal', 'Volume only matters for selling', 'There is no concern; volume does not affect breakouts', 'Thin-volume breakouts are more prone to failing and snapping back'], answer: 3, explain: 'Without strong participation, a breakout is more likely to be a fakeout.' },
          { q: 'After a months-long slide, a stock plunges early on 12x normal volume, then recovers to close near its high. A trader might call this...', options: ['A possible selling climax', 'A golden cross', 'A buying climax', 'A low-volume pullback'], answer: 0, explain: 'Huge volume, panic, then a long lower wick after a long decline is the classic capitulation picture. It still needs follow-through to confirm.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-moving-averages',
      title: 'Moving Averages: Smoothing the Noise',
      summary: 'SMA vs EMA, the famous 20/50/200, golden and death crosses, and the built-in lag nobody escapes.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Turning a jagged line into a smooth one', html: `<p>Daily prices jump around. Up 2%, down 1.5%, up 0.3%. It is hard to see the trend through all that jitter. A <span class="term" data-def="A line showing the average closing price over a rolling window of recent periods, updated each period.">moving average</span> fixes that by averaging the last N closing prices and drawing the result as a smooth line.</p><p>"Moving" means the window slides forward. Each new day, the newest close enters the average and the oldest one drops out.</p><p>The simplest version is the <span class="term" data-def="Simple moving average: add up the last N closes and divide by N. Every day counts equally.">SMA</span>, or simple moving average. Add up the last N closes, divide by N. Done.</p><p>A 5-day SMA of closes $10, $12, $11, $13 and $14:</p><p>10 + 12 + 11 + 13 + 14 = 60. Then 60 / 5 = <strong>$12</strong>.</p><p>Tomorrow, if the stock closes at $15, the $10 drops off and the $15 joins: 12 + 11 + 13 + 14 + 15 = 65, and 65 / 5 = <strong>$13</strong>. The line ticks up.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "An average is just a sum divided by a count. You have been doing this since your first report card." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Closes over five days: $40, $42, $41, $44, $43. What is the 5-day SMA?', answer: 42, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '40 + 42 + 41 + 44 + 43 = 210. Then 210 / 5 = $42.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'EMA: recent days count more', html: `<p>The SMA treats a close from 49 days ago exactly the same as yesterday's. Some traders think that is silly, since yesterday is more relevant. Enter the <span class="term" data-def="Exponential moving average: a moving average that gives more weight to recent prices, so it reacts faster than an SMA.">EMA</span>, or exponential moving average.</p><p>The EMA uses a weighting multiplier of <strong>2 / (N + 1)</strong>. For a 9-period EMA that is 2 / 10 = 0.2. Each new day:</p><p><strong>New EMA = old EMA + 0.2 x (today's close - old EMA)</strong></p><p>So if yesterday's 9-EMA was $50 and today closes at $56, the new EMA is 50 + 0.2 x 6 = <strong>$51.20</strong>. The EMA moved 20% of the way toward today's price.</p><p>Result: EMAs hug price more tightly and turn faster than SMAs of the same length. That is great for catching moves early, and worse for getting faked out by random wiggles. Neither is "better." It is a trade-off between <span class="hl">speed and smoothness</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'moving-averages', caption: 'A faster moving average hugs price closely. A slower one is smoother and trails behind. Where they cross is what crossover traders watch.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The famous lengths: 20, 50, 200', html: `<p>You can use any length, but a few are so popular that they become self-fulfilling to some degree, because so many traders watch them:</p><ul><li><strong>20-day</strong>: about one month of trading. A short-term trend gauge, popular with swing traders. (It is also the middle line of Bollinger Bands.)</li><li><strong>50-day</strong>: about ten weeks. The medium-term trend. Many growth investors watch whether leading stocks hold above it.</li><li><strong>200-day</strong>: about 40 weeks, close to a year of trading. The big one. Financial news regularly reports whether major indexes are above or below their 200-day average as a rough bull or bear gauge.</li></ul><p>Day traders use the same idea on intraday charts, such as the 9 and 21 EMA on a 5-minute chart. That is exactly what Bolt's EMA Crossover strategy watches: when the 9 EMA crosses above the 21 EMA it looks for longs, and when it crosses below it looks to exit or stand aside.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "My EMA Crossover rules: 9 crosses above 21, consider a long. Crosses back below, get out. No feelings involved." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Golden cross and death cross', html: `<p>Two crossovers have dramatic names and get headlines:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="When the 50-day moving average crosses above the 200-day moving average. Often read as a bullish long-term signal.">Golden cross</span>: the 50-day crosses <span class="up">above</span> the 200-day. Read as a sign the medium-term trend has turned up relative to the long-term one.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="When the 50-day moving average crosses below the 200-day moving average. Often read as a bearish long-term signal.">Death cross</span>: the 50-day crosses <span class="down">below</span> the 200-day. Read as a sign of a weakening trend.</li></ul><p>They sound terrifying or thrilling, but here is the catch: because both averages are slow, the cross usually happens <strong>well after</strong> the move has started. By the time a death cross prints, the stock has typically already fallen a good amount, and sometimes it bounces right after. Golden crosses can arrive after much of a rally is already done. They are better at describing the trend than predicting the next move.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: "A stock's 50-day SMA just crossed above its 200-day SMA. What is this called?", options: ['A death cross', 'A golden cross', 'A bearish engulfing', 'A squeeze'], answer: 1, explain: 'Golden cross = 50-day crossing above 200-day. Death cross is the reverse.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Moving averages as dynamic support', html: `<p>Horizontal support sits at a fixed price. A moving average is a <span class="hl">support line that moves with the trend</span>, which is why traders call it "dynamic" support or resistance.</p><p>In a strong uptrend, pullbacks often stall near a rising moving average, like the 20-day or the 50-day, before the trend resumes. Plenty of traders buy those touches. In a downtrend, rallies often stall near a falling average.</p><p>Why would a line on a screen do anything? Partly because so many people watch the same popular averages and act near them, and partly because a moving average roughly tracks the "typical" price buyers paid recently. When price dips to it, it is back to fair-ish value for recent buyers.</p><p>Just like horizontal levels, these are zones. Price slices through moving averages all the time, especially in choppy markets.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The lag you cannot escape', html: `<p>Every moving average is built from <em>past</em> prices, so it always trails behind. That is called <span class="term" data-def="The delay between a price change and when an indicator reflects it.">lag</span>. A 200-day SMA is essentially telling you the average price over most of the past year.</p><p>Longer averages lag more and give fewer, slower signals. Shorter averages lag less and give more signals, including lots of false ones. There is no length that gives early signals <em>and</em> no false alarms. Anyone selling you that is selling you something.</p><p>Lag hurts most in ranges. When price chops sideways, a fast and a slow average keep crossing back and forth, producing a string of losing "signals." This is called a <strong>whipsaw</strong>, and every crossover strategy, including Bolt's, suffers from it. Watching Bolt's trade log on a sideways week is a great way to see whipsaws in action.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Moving averages tell you where price has been. They do not know where it is going. Respect the lag." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A 9-period EMA will usually react faster to a sudden price jump than a 9-period SMA.', answer: true, explain: 'The EMA weights recent prices more heavily, so it moves toward a new price faster than an SMA of the same length.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each term to its meaning.', pairs: [
          { left: 'SMA', right: 'Equal-weighted average of the last N closes' },
          { left: 'EMA', right: 'Average that weights recent closes more heavily' },
          { left: 'Golden cross', right: '50-day crosses above 200-day' },
          { left: 'Whipsaw', right: 'Repeated false crossovers in a choppy market' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'A simple trend filter', html: `<p>Many traders use one rule before anything else: if price is above a rising 50-day or 200-day average, only consider long setups; if below a falling one, stand aside or consider other approaches. It will not catch every turn, but it keeps you from fighting the main trend, which is where a lot of beginner losses come from.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Closes: $20, $22, $24, $26. What is the 4-day SMA?', options: ['$22', '$24', '$23', '$26'], answer: 2, explain: '20 + 22 + 24 + 26 = 92. Then 92 / 4 = $23.' },
          { q: 'What is the main difference between an EMA and an SMA?', options: ['The EMA weights recent prices more heavily', 'The SMA only uses volume', 'The EMA ignores closing prices', 'They are identical'], answer: 0, explain: 'EMAs give more weight to recent data, so they react faster.' },
          { q: 'What is a death cross?', options: ['Price falls 10% in one day', 'The 9 EMA crosses above the 21 EMA', 'RSI drops below 30', 'The 50-day average crosses below the 200-day average'], answer: 3, explain: 'Death cross = 50-day crossing below 200-day. It usually arrives well after the decline started.' },
          { q: 'Why do crossover strategies often lose money in sideways markets?', options: ['Moving averages stop updating in ranges', 'Averages keep crossing back and forth, creating repeated false signals', 'Exchanges halt trading in ranges', 'Ranges always have zero volume'], answer: 1, explain: 'Whipsaws: in chop, crossovers keep firing and reversing, stacking up small losses.' },
          { q: "Yesterday's 9-EMA was $100. Today the stock closes at $110. Using a multiplier of 0.2, what is today's 9-EMA?", options: ['$102', '$110', '$105', '$101'], answer: 0, explain: '100 + 0.2 x (110 - 100) = 100 + 2 = $102.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-rsi-momentum',
      title: 'RSI: Measuring the Speed of Price',
      summary: 'The 14-period RSI, the 70/30 zones, divergences, and why overbought stocks can keep getting more overbought.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'bolt',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Not where, but how fast', html: `<p>So far you have looked at <em>where</em> price is: above support, below resistance, above a moving average. <span class="term" data-def="The speed and strength of a price move. Strong momentum means price is moving quickly in one direction.">Momentum</span> indicators ask a different question: <strong>how hard is price moving?</strong></p><p>Think of a car. Its position tells you where it is. Its speed tells you how fast it is getting somewhere. A car slowing down on a highway on-ramp has not reversed yet, but you can tell something is changing.</p><p>The most popular momentum indicator is the <span class="term" data-def="Relative Strength Index: a 0 to 100 momentum oscillator comparing the size of recent gains to recent losses.">Relative Strength Index</span>, or RSI. J. Welles Wilder Jr. introduced it in his 1978 book on trading systems, and it is now built into virtually every charting platform. It squeezes recent price action into a single number between <strong>0 and 100</strong> that usually sits in a panel under the chart.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "RSI is a speedometer for price. It does not tell you the destination." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'How RSI is calculated', html: `<p>RSI compares the average size of <span class="up">up</span> moves to the average size of <span class="down">down</span> moves over a lookback window. The default is <strong>14 periods</strong> (14 days on a daily chart).</p><ol><li>Average gain over the last 14 periods (counting down days as 0 gain)</li><li>Average loss over the last 14 periods (counting up days as 0 loss)</li><li><strong>RS = average gain / average loss</strong></li><li><strong>RSI = 100 - 100 / (1 + RS)</strong></li></ol><p>If average gains equal average losses, RS = 1 and RSI = 100 - 100/2 = <strong>50</strong>, perfectly balanced. If gains are twice the losses, RS = 2 and RSI = 100 - 100/3, about <strong>66.7</strong>. If there were no losses at all, RSI hits 100.</p><p>Wilder's original version smooths these averages so each new day nudges them rather than replacing them, which is why your app's RSI may differ slightly from a quick hand calculation. The intuition is what matters: <span class="hl">RSI rises when gains dominate and falls when losses dominate</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Over 14 days, the average gain is $3 and the average loss is $1. What is the RSI? (Round to the nearest whole number.)', answer: 75, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '', explain: 'RS = 3 / 1 = 3. RSI = 100 - 100 / (1 + 3) = 100 - 25 = 75.' },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'rsi-zones', caption: 'RSI swings between 0 and 100. Above 70 is the traditional overbought zone, below 30 is oversold, and 50 is the midline between bullish and bearish momentum.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The 70/30 zones', html: `<p>Wilder suggested two reference lines:</p><ul><li><strong>Above 70</strong>: <span class="term" data-def="A condition where price has risen quickly enough that RSI is above about 70. Not a sell signal by itself.">overbought</span>. Price has been rising fast relative to its recent pullbacks.</li><li><strong>Below 30</strong>: <span class="term" data-def="A condition where price has fallen quickly enough that RSI is below about 30. Not a buy signal by itself.">oversold</span>. Price has been falling fast relative to its recent bounces.</li></ul><p>Some traders use 80/20 for stricter readings, especially on volatile stocks. The midline, 50, is also useful: momentum above 50 leans bullish, below 50 leans bearish.</p><p>Here is how beginners misread it. Maya sees RSI at 74 and thinks "overbought, so it must drop." But overbought just means <em>it went up fast</em>. Going up fast is what strong stocks do. It is a description of the recent past, not an order to sell.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Overbought can stay overbought', html: `<p>In a powerful uptrend, RSI can sit above 70 for weeks while the stock keeps climbing. Traders who short every time RSI crosses 70 in a strong trend get flattened repeatedly. Same in reverse: in a brutal downtrend, "oversold" stocks can keep getting more oversold.</p><p>A more useful way to think about it is <span class="hl">RSI behaves differently depending on the trend</span>:</p><ul><li>In uptrends, RSI tends to stay higher. Pullbacks often bottom with RSI around 40 to 50 instead of reaching 30, and peaks can push above 70 or 80.</li><li>In downtrends, RSI tends to stay lower. Bounces often stall near 50 to 60, and lows can go below 30 again and again.</li></ul><p>So a trend trader might treat an RSI dip toward 40 in an uptrend as a possible pullback entry, not wait for a 30 that may never come. Bolt's RSI Swing strategy uses RSI as part of its rules; check its rule card on My Desk to see exactly which levels and filters it uses.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Shorting a stock just because RSI says 'overbought' is like stepping in front of a train because it is going 'too fast.'" } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'An RSI reading above 70 is a reliable signal to sell or short a stock.', answer: false, explain: 'Overbought only means price rose quickly. In strong uptrends RSI can stay above 70 for long stretches while price keeps rising.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Divergence: when price and momentum disagree', html: `<p>One of the more interesting RSI signals is <span class="term" data-def="When price and an indicator move in opposite directions, such as price making a higher high while RSI makes a lower high.">divergence</span>.</p><ul><li><strong>Bearish divergence:</strong> price makes a <em>higher high</em>, but RSI makes a <em>lower high</em>. The stock went higher, but with less force behind it. The car is still going forward, but someone is easing off the gas.</li><li><strong>Bullish divergence:</strong> price makes a <em>lower low</em>, but RSI makes a <em>higher low</em>. Selling pressure is weakening even as price dips.</li></ul><p>Leo watches a stock hit $120, then later push to $124. RSI peaked at 78 the first time and only 66 the second time. That is bearish divergence: the second push had less momentum.</p><p>Does that mean the stock will fall? Not necessarily. Divergences can stack up several times before anything happens, and sometimes nothing happens at all. Treat divergence as a <strong>yellow light</strong>, then wait for price itself to confirm, such as a break of a recent swing low.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Price makes a lower low, but RSI makes a higher low. What is this?', options: ['Bearish divergence', 'Bullish divergence', 'A golden cross', 'An overbought signal'], answer: 1, explain: 'Lower low in price + higher low in RSI = bullish divergence. Downward momentum is fading. It still needs price confirmation.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: oversold means cheap', html: `<p>RSI measures <em>speed</em>, not <em>value</em>. A stock with RSI at 22 is not "on sale." It is falling fast, maybe for a very good reason like a failed product or a fraud investigation. Whether a company is cheap is a fundamentals question (earnings, cash flow, balance sheet), which you studied earlier in this unit. RSI cannot answer it.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each RSI situation to its usual reading.', pairs: [
          { left: 'RSI above 70', right: 'Price has risen quickly; overbought' },
          { left: 'RSI below 30', right: 'Price has fallen quickly; oversold' },
          { left: 'Price higher high, RSI lower high', right: 'Bearish divergence' },
          { left: 'RSI holding above 40 on pullbacks', right: 'Typical of a strong uptrend' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Using RSI without getting burned', html: `<p>A sensible RSI checklist looks like this:</p><ol><li><strong>Trend first.</strong> Decide whether the stock is in an uptrend, downtrend or range before you look at RSI at all.</li><li><strong>In ranges,</strong> the classic 70/30 readings work better, because price really does tend to swing between a floor and a ceiling.</li><li><strong>In trends,</strong> use RSI to time pullbacks in the trend direction, not to fight the trend.</li><li><strong>Divergences</strong> are warnings. Wait for price to confirm.</li><li><strong>Combine,</strong> do not rely on one tool. RSI plus a support level plus volume is far more convincing than RSI alone.</li></ol><p>And remember: RSI is calculated entirely from closing prices. It cannot see anything price has not already shown. It is a different way of looking at the same data, not new information.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Trend first, then RSI. Doing it in the other order is how you end up arguing with a freight train." } },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is the default lookback period for RSI?', options: ['9 periods', '50 periods', '200 periods', '14 periods'], answer: 3, explain: 'Wilder used 14 periods, and it remains the default on most platforms.' },
          { q: 'If average gains equal average losses over the lookback, RSI equals...', options: ['50', '0', '100', '70'], answer: 0, explain: 'RS = 1, so RSI = 100 - 100/2 = 50.' },
          { q: 'In a strong uptrend, RSI has been above 70 for three weeks and price keeps rising. What is the best interpretation?', options: ['The stock must crash tomorrow', 'RSI is broken', 'Strong momentum; overbought can persist in powerful trends', 'It is now oversold'], answer: 2, explain: 'Overbought describes fast gains. Strong trends can stay overbought for a long time.' },
          { q: 'Price makes a higher high while RSI makes a lower high. This is...', options: ['Bullish divergence', 'Bearish divergence', 'A death cross', 'A selling climax'], answer: 1, explain: 'Higher price high with weaker RSI high = bearish divergence: momentum is fading.' },
          { q: 'Dev buys a stock because its RSI is 18, saying "it is so cheap now." What is the flaw?', options: ['RSI measures speed of moves, not whether a company is cheap', 'RSI cannot go below 20', 'Oversold always means a bounce within one day', 'He should have waited for RSI to hit 0'], answer: 0, explain: 'Low RSI means price fell fast. Value is a fundamentals question. A stock can fall fast because the business is in real trouble.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-macd',
      title: 'MACD: Two Averages Having a Conversation',
      summary: 'The 12/26 EMA difference, the 9-period signal line, the histogram and crossovers, explained without the jargon fog.',
      minutes: 13,
      icon: 'chart',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'A scary acronym for a simple idea', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Moving Average Convergence Divergence: an indicator built from the gap between a 12-period and a 26-period EMA.">MACD</span> stands for Moving Average Convergence Divergence. People usually say it "mack-dee." It was developed by Gerald Appel in the late 1970s and is now one of the most widely used indicators on the planet.</p><p>The name sounds like a physics exam. The idea is not. You already know that a fast EMA reacts quickly and a slow EMA reacts slowly. MACD simply measures <strong>the gap between them</strong>.</p><ul><li>When the fast EMA pulls away above the slow one, momentum is building upward.</li><li>When the two squeeze together, momentum is fading. That is the "convergence."</li><li>When the fast EMA pulls away below, momentum is building downward. Moving apart is the "divergence."</li></ul><p>MACD turns that relationship into a line you can watch in its own panel under the chart.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Moving Average Convergence Divergence. Four words, one subtraction. Academics love a long name." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The three pieces', html: `<p>MACD has three parts, all built from closing prices with the standard settings <strong>12, 26, 9</strong>:</p><ol><li><strong>MACD line</strong> = 12-period EMA minus 26-period EMA. Positive when the fast average is above the slow one, negative when below.</li><li><strong>Signal line</strong> = a 9-period EMA <em>of the MACD line itself</em>. A smoothed, slower version of the MACD line.</li><li><strong>Histogram</strong> = MACD line minus signal line, drawn as bars. Bars above zero when MACD is above its signal line, below zero when under it.</li></ol><p>Quick example. The 12-EMA is $105.40 and the 26-EMA is $103.90. The MACD line is 105.40 - 103.90 = <strong>1.50</strong>. If the signal line is 1.20, the histogram bar is 1.50 - 1.20 = <strong>0.30</strong>, a small positive bar.</p><p>MACD is measured in price units (dollars, for a stock), so a "big" MACD on a $900 stock and on a $9 stock are not comparable.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'macd', caption: 'Top: price. Bottom: the MACD line, the slower signal line, and the histogram showing the gap between them. Watch the histogram shrink before the lines cross.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'The 12-period EMA is $48.60 and the 26-period EMA is $47.10. What is the MACD line value?', answer: 1.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '', explain: 'MACD = 12 EMA - 26 EMA = 48.60 - 47.10 = 1.50.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Signal-line crossovers', html: `<p>The most common MACD signal is the <span class="term" data-def="When the MACD line crosses above or below its 9-period signal line.">signal-line crossover</span>:</p><ul><li><strong>Bullish crossover:</strong> the MACD line crosses <span class="up">above</span> the signal line. Upward momentum is picking up. The histogram flips from negative to positive.</li><li><strong>Bearish crossover:</strong> the MACD line crosses <span class="down">below</span> the signal line. Momentum is rolling over. The histogram flips from positive to negative.</li></ul><p>These crossovers happen often, which means plenty of false signals, especially in choppy markets. Many traders give more weight to crossovers that agree with the bigger trend. A bullish crossover while the stock is above a rising 200-day average is more interesting than one in the middle of a long downtrend.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Zero-line crossovers', html: `<p>The other big signal is the MACD line crossing <strong>zero</strong>.</p><p>Since MACD = 12 EMA - 26 EMA, a MACD of exactly zero means the two EMAs are equal. So when MACD crosses above zero, the 12 EMA has just crossed above the 26 EMA. That is the same idea as the moving-average crossovers from the last lesson, just viewed in a different panel.</p><ul><li><strong>Above zero:</strong> short-term average above long-term average. Momentum leans bullish.</li><li><strong>Below zero:</strong> short-term average below long-term average. Momentum leans bearish.</li></ul><p>Zero-line crosses are slower than signal-line crosses, so they give fewer, later signals. Some traders use the signal line for timing and the zero line as a trend filter: only take bullish signal-line crosses when MACD is above zero, for example.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "MACD above zero means the 12 EMA is above the 26 EMA. Same math, different chart. I like it when indicators admit what they are." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'The MACD line just crossed from -0.40 to +0.10. What does that tell you about the moving averages?', options: ['The 12-period EMA just crossed above the 26-period EMA', 'RSI just crossed 70', 'The 50-day crossed the 200-day', 'Volume doubled'], answer: 0, explain: 'MACD = 12 EMA - 26 EMA. Going from negative to positive means the 12 EMA moved from below to above the 26 EMA.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Reading the histogram', html: `<p>The histogram is the most underrated piece. It shows the <em>gap</em> between MACD and its signal line, which means it shows whether momentum is <strong>accelerating or decelerating</strong>.</p><ul><li>Positive bars getting <strong>taller</strong>: upward momentum is accelerating.</li><li>Positive bars getting <strong>shorter</strong>: still bullish, but momentum is fading. A bearish crossover may be coming.</li><li>Negative bars getting shorter: selling momentum is easing.</li></ul><p>Because the histogram shrinks before the lines actually cross, some traders watch it as an early warning. Earlier warning means more false warnings too. There is always a trade-off between speed and reliability.</p><p>MACD also shows divergence, just like RSI. If price makes a higher high but the MACD line makes a lower high, upward momentum is weakening.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'When the histogram bars are positive but getting shorter, the MACD line is still above the signal line.', answer: true, explain: 'Positive bars mean MACD is above its signal line. Shrinking bars mean the gap is narrowing, so momentum is fading, but no bearish crossover has happened yet.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Lag on top of lag', html: `<p>MACD is built from EMAs (which lag), and the signal line is an EMA of that (which lags more). By design, MACD will never catch the exact top or bottom. In fast reversals, such as a stock collapsing on bad earnings, MACD can still look bullish for a day or two after price has already cratered. Use it to understand momentum, and use price levels and stops to manage risk.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'MACD vs RSI: cousins, not twins', html: `<p>MACD and RSI both measure momentum, so beginners often stack them and feel doubly sure. Careful: they are built from the <em>same closing prices</em>, so when they agree, it is partly because they are looking at the same thing.</p><p>The useful differences:</p><ul><li><strong>RSI is bounded</strong> between 0 and 100, so it has fixed overbought and oversold zones.</li><li><strong>MACD is unbounded.</strong> It has no fixed "too high" level, so it is better at showing trend direction and shifts in momentum than at calling extremes.</li></ul><p>Leo tried a simple rule on My Desk: buy a bullish MACD crossover only when price is also above its 50-day average and RSI is above 50. Over a month of paper trades it skipped a lot of chop that a crossover-only rule would have taken. That does not prove the rule works forever, but it shows the idea: each extra filter should answer a <em>different</em> question, like trend, location or volume.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: "Three momentum indicators agreeing is not three opinions. It is one opinion in three outfits." } },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Order the steps to build MACD from scratch.', items: ['Calculate the 12-period EMA of closing prices', 'Calculate the 26-period EMA of closing prices', 'Subtract to get the MACD line (12 EMA - 26 EMA)', 'Take a 9-period EMA of the MACD line to get the signal line', 'Subtract signal from MACD to get the histogram'], explain: 'Each piece is built on the one before. The histogram is the last step because it needs both the MACD line and the signal line.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'MACD in one breath', cards: [
          { front: 'MACD line', back: '12-period EMA minus 26-period EMA.' },
          { front: 'Signal line', back: '9-period EMA of the MACD line. Slower and smoother.' },
          { front: 'Histogram', back: 'MACD minus signal. Shows if momentum is speeding up or slowing down.' },
          { front: 'Zero line', back: 'Where the 12 and 26 EMAs are equal. Above means short-term average is higher.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is the MACD line?', options: ['The 9-period SMA of price', 'RSI minus 50', 'The 12-period EMA minus the 26-period EMA', 'The 50-day minus the 200-day SMA'], answer: 2, explain: 'Standard MACD = 12 EMA - 26 EMA.' },
          { q: 'What is the signal line?', options: ['A 9-period EMA of the MACD line', 'The 26-period EMA of price', 'A horizontal line at zero', 'The daily volume average'], answer: 0, explain: 'The signal line smooths the MACD line with a 9-period EMA.' },
          { q: 'MACD line = 2.10, signal line = 2.60. What is the histogram value, and what does it suggest?', options: ['+0.50, bullish acceleration', '-0.50, MACD is below its signal line', '4.70, very bullish', '0, no momentum'], answer: 1, explain: '2.10 - 2.60 = -0.50. MACD is under its signal line, so short-term momentum is weaker than its recent average.' },
          { q: 'Why does MACD tend to give late signals in sharp reversals?', options: ['It uses volume, which updates slowly', 'It only updates once a week', 'Exchanges delay the data', 'It is built from EMAs, and the signal line is an average of an average'], answer: 3, explain: 'Stacked moving averages add lag. That is the price of smoothness.' },
          { q: 'Maya only takes bullish MACD signal-line crossovers when MACD is above zero. What is she using the zero line for?', options: ['As a trend filter, trading only when the short-term average is above the long-term one', 'To measure volume', 'To find support levels', 'To calculate RSI'], answer: 0, explain: 'Above zero means the 12 EMA is above the 26 EMA, so she is trading with the prevailing momentum.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-bollinger-vwap',
      title: 'Bollinger Bands & VWAP',
      summary: 'Bands that breathe with volatility, squeezes that come before big moves, and VWAP, the intraday line big institutions are graded against.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bands that breathe', html: `<p>Moving averages tell you the trend. But they say nothing about how <em>wild</em> the price action is. A stock calmly drifting and a stock lurching 5% a day can have the same 20-day average.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="An indicator made of a 20-period simple moving average with bands 2 standard deviations above and below it.">Bollinger Bands</span>, created by John Bollinger in the 1980s, add that missing piece. They have three lines:</p><ul><li><strong>Middle band:</strong> a 20-period simple moving average</li><li><strong>Upper band:</strong> the middle band <strong>plus 2 standard deviations</strong></li><li><strong>Lower band:</strong> the middle band <strong>minus 2 standard deviations</strong></li></ul><p><span class="term" data-def="A statistic measuring how spread out numbers are from their average. Bigger means more scattered.">Standard deviation</span> measures how spread out the last 20 closes are around their average. When prices are scattered, it is big, and the bands widen. When prices are tightly bunched, it is small, and the bands pinch together. The bands literally breathe in and out with volatility.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "Standard deviation sounds intimidating. It just means 'how far from average do things usually wander?'" } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A 20-day SMA is $80 and the standard deviation of the last 20 closes is $3. Where is the upper Bollinger Band?', answer: 86, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'Upper band = SMA + 2 x standard deviation = 80 + 2 x 3 = $86. The lower band would be $74.' },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'bollinger', caption: 'The bands hug price in quiet periods (the squeeze) and flare wide during big moves. Notice how a tight squeeze is often followed by expansion.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Touching a band is not a signal', html: `<p>Beginners often treat the upper band as "sell" and the lower band as "buy." That works sometimes in a sideways range. In a trend it can be a disaster.</p><p>In a strong uptrend, price can <span class="hl">walk the upper band</span>, tagging or riding along it day after day. Touching the upper band there is a sign of strength, not a top. The same goes for a stock walking down the lower band in a selloff.</p><p>John Bollinger himself has repeatedly said that tags of the bands are not buy or sell signals on their own. A useful way to think about it: the bands define what is <em>relatively</em> high or low compared with recent volatility. What you do with that depends on the trend and other evidence.</p><p>Where bands do shine is the mean-reversion idea in ranges: in a sideways market, a stretch to the lower band near support, followed by a close back inside, can be a reasonable place to look for a bounce toward the middle band. Context decides.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The squeeze', html: `<p>Volatility moves in cycles. Quiet periods tend to be followed by loud ones, and vice versa. Bollinger Bands make this visible.</p><p>When the bands pinch unusually close together, traders call it a <span class="term" data-def="A period when Bollinger Bands narrow sharply because volatility is low. Often followed by a larger move in one direction.">squeeze</span>. Volatility is compressed, like a spring being pushed down. Squeezes are often followed by an expansion: a sharp move as the bands flare open.</p><p>The catch: <strong>the squeeze does not tell you which direction</strong>. The spring can shoot up or down. Many traders wait for price to break out of the squeeze with a close outside a band, ideally on rising volume, before acting, and some deliberately expect a fake move first.</p><p>Bolt's Bollinger Squeeze strategy on My Desk is built around this idea: it watches for unusually narrow bands and then waits for a breakout to choose a direction. Watch its trade log to see how often the first break is real and how often it reverses.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: "Squeeze detected. Direction unknown. I wait for the break. Guessing is not one of my rules." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A stock\'s Bollinger Bands have narrowed to their tightest point in six months. What is the most accurate takeaway?', options: ['The stock will definitely rally', 'The stock will definitely fall', 'Volatility is compressed, and a larger move may come, direction unknown', 'The indicator is broken'], answer: 2, explain: 'A squeeze signals low volatility that often precedes expansion. It says nothing about direction by itself.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'VWAP: the institutional scoreboard', html: `<p>Now switch to the intraday world. <span class="term" data-def="Volume-Weighted Average Price: the average price paid for a stock today, weighted by how many shares traded at each price.">VWAP</span>, or volume-weighted average price, is the average price of every share traded so far today, weighted by volume.</p><p>Simple example: 100 shares trade at $10, then 300 shares trade at $12.</p><p>Total dollars: 100 x 10 + 300 x 12 = $4,600. Total shares: 400. VWAP = 4,600 / 400 = <strong>$11.50</strong>.</p><p>A plain average of the two prices would be $11, but more shares traded at $12, so VWAP leans toward it. (Charting apps usually use each bar's typical price, the average of its high, low and close, instead of every single trade, but the idea is identical.)</p><p>Two key facts:</p><ul><li><strong>VWAP resets every day.</strong> It starts fresh at the open of each regular session. It is an intraday tool.</li><li><strong>Institutions use it as a benchmark.</strong> A fund buying a million shares over a day is often judged on whether it beat VWAP. Buying below VWAP is a good execution; above it, not so good.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Today 200 shares traded at $50 and 600 shares traded at $54. What is the VWAP?', answer: 53, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '(200 x 50 + 600 x 54) / 800 = (10,000 + 32,400) / 800 = 42,400 / 800 = $53.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Trading around VWAP', html: `<p>Because so many big players care about VWAP, it often behaves like a magnet and a dividing line during the day:</p><ul><li><strong>Price above VWAP:</strong> the average buyer today is in profit. Intraday traders read this as buyers being in control.</li><li><strong>Price below VWAP:</strong> the average buyer today is underwater. Sellers have the upper hand.</li><li><strong>Reclaiming VWAP:</strong> a stock that was below all morning and pushes back above it is often watched as a shift in intraday control.</li></ul><p>Bolt's VWAP Mean Reversion strategy bets that when price stretches far from VWAP, it tends to snap back toward it. That idea works best on calm, range-bound days. On a strong trend day, price can run away from VWAP and never look back, which is exactly when mean reversion gets hurt.</p><p>Since VWAP resets daily, it means little for long-term investors. Leo, who holds index funds for years, can safely ignore it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Mean reversion works until a trend day shows up. Then 'it'll come back to VWAP' becomes a very expensive sentence." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'VWAP keeps accumulating across many days, so it is mainly used by long-term investors.', answer: false, explain: 'Standard VWAP resets at the start of each session. It is an intraday benchmark, mainly used by day traders and institutional execution desks.' },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each tool to what it does.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Middle Bollinger Band', right: '20-period simple moving average' },
          { left: 'Upper Bollinger Band', right: 'Middle band plus 2 standard deviations' },
          { left: 'Bollinger squeeze', right: 'Unusually low volatility, bands pinched together' },
          { left: 'VWAP', right: 'Volume-weighted average price for today, resets daily' },
        ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Bands and VWAP vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Standard deviation', back: 'How far prices usually wander from their average. Bigger means more volatile.' },
          { front: 'Walking the band', back: 'Price riding along the upper or lower band during a strong trend.' },
          { front: 'Squeeze', back: 'Bands pinched tight because volatility is low. Often precedes a bigger move.' },
          { front: 'VWAP', back: 'Average price paid today, weighted by shares traded. Starts over each session.' },
          { front: 'Mean reversion', back: 'The idea that a stretched price tends to drift back toward an average.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Try it on My Desk', html: `<p>Open a liquid stock on a 5-minute chart and turn on both VWAP and Bollinger Bands. Over a few sessions, note which days price kept snapping back to VWAP (range days) and which days it never returned (trend days). Learning to tell those two day types apart early is one of the most valuable intraday skills there is, and one no single indicator can do for you.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What are the standard Bollinger Band settings?', options: ['9 EMA plus or minus 1%', '20-period SMA plus or minus 2 standard deviations', '50-day SMA plus or minus $2', '14-period RSI plus or minus 30'], answer: 1, explain: 'Default: 20-period SMA middle, bands 2 standard deviations above and below.' },
          { q: 'What happens to Bollinger Bands when volatility rises?', options: ['They narrow', 'They disappear', 'They stay exactly the same width', 'They widen'], answer: 3, explain: 'Higher volatility = larger standard deviation = wider bands.' },
          { q: 'A stock in a strong uptrend keeps closing near its upper Bollinger Band. What is the best reading?', options: ['It is walking the band, a sign of strength in a trend', 'It must reverse tomorrow', 'It is in a squeeze', 'It is below VWAP'], answer: 0, explain: 'In strong trends, price can ride the upper band. A tag is not automatically a sell signal.' },
          { q: 'When does standard VWAP reset?', options: ['Every hour', 'Every week', 'At the start of each trading session', 'Only after earnings'], answer: 2, explain: 'VWAP is calculated from the session open and resets daily.' },
          { q: 'Why do many institutions care about VWAP?', options: ['The SEC requires all trades at VWAP', 'It measures a company\'s fair value', 'It is the only price available after hours', 'Their large orders are often judged on whether they bought or sold better than VWAP'], answer: 3, explain: 'VWAP is a common execution benchmark for big orders spread across the day.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
    {
      id: 'stocks-4-patterns-and-limits',
      title: 'Chart Patterns & the Limits of TA',
      summary: 'Head and shoulders, double tops, flags, triangles and cups, plus the honest truth about what chart reading can and cannot do.',
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'lightbulb',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Shapes the crowd draws', html: `<p>Over many decades, traders noticed that certain shapes keep showing up on charts. These <span class="term" data-def="Recognizable shapes formed by price over days to months, used to frame possible continuations or reversals.">chart patterns</span> are just bigger versions of what you already know: swing highs, swing lows, support and resistance, arranged in a recognizable way.</p><p>Patterns fall into two families:</p><ul><li><strong>Reversal patterns</strong> suggest a trend may be ending: head and shoulders, double tops and bottoms.</li><li><strong>Continuation patterns</strong> suggest a trend is pausing before carrying on: flags, many triangles, cup and handle.</li></ul><p>Every pattern has a <span class="hl">trigger level</span>, the price where the pattern is considered complete. Before the trigger breaks, it is just a possible pattern. Many shapes that look like they are forming a famous pattern never finish it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Patterns are swing highs and lows wearing costumes. You already know the ingredients." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'chart-patterns', caption: 'Head and shoulders, double top and bottom, flag, triangle, and cup and handle. Find each pattern\'s trigger line: the neckline, the flag edge, or the handle high.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Head and shoulders, double tops and bottoms', html: `<p><strong>Head and shoulders</strong> is a topping pattern: a peak (left shoulder), a higher peak (head), then a lower peak (right shoulder). The lows between the peaks form the <span class="term" data-def="The line connecting the lows between the shoulders and head. A close below it completes a head and shoulders top.">neckline</span>. The pattern completes when price breaks below the neckline. Notice what that means structurally: the right shoulder is a lower high, and the neckline break is a lower low. The uptrend has failed.</p><p>The <strong>inverse head and shoulders</strong> is the same thing upside down, a possible bottom.</p><p>A <strong>double top</strong> looks like an M: two peaks at about the same price with a dip between. It completes when price breaks below that dip. A <strong>double bottom</strong> is a W, completing on a break above the middle peak.</p><p>Traders often estimate a <span class="hl">measured move</span>: take the height of the pattern and project it from the breakout point. If the head is at $60 and the neckline at $50, the height is $10, so a neckline break suggests a rough target near $40. It is a rule of thumb, not a promise.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A double bottom has two lows at $30 and a middle peak at $36. Using a measured move, what is the rough target after a breakout above $36?', answer: 42, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: 'Height = 36 - 30 = $6. Project from the breakout: 36 + 6 = $42. Treat it as a rough guide, not a guarantee.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Flags, triangles, cup and handle', html: `<ul><li><strong>Flag:</strong> a sharp rally (the flagpole) followed by a short, tight drift sideways or slightly down (the flag). A break above the flag suggests the trend is resuming. Bear flags are the mirror image. A <em>pennant</em> is a flag shaped like a tiny symmetrical triangle.</li><li><strong>Triangles:</strong> price swings in a narrowing range. An <em>ascending triangle</em> has a flat top and rising lows, meaning buyers keep stepping in higher. A <em>descending triangle</em> has a flat bottom and falling highs. A <em>symmetrical triangle</em> has both lines converging. The break usually comes with a jump in volume.</li><li><strong>Cup and handle:</strong> a rounded, U-shaped base (the cup), followed by a small pullback (the handle), then a breakout above the handle high. William O'Neil popularized it as a key base pattern in his CANSLIM approach, ideally with a big volume surge on the breakout.</li></ul><p>Notice that every continuation pattern is just a pause, a mini-range inside a trend, with a breakout level on top.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "My Momentum Breakout and CANSLIM rules both look for tight bases breaking out on heavy volume. Patterns become code." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each pattern to its shape.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Double top', right: 'M shape: two peaks near the same price' },
          { left: 'Head and shoulders', right: 'Three peaks, the middle one highest' },
          { left: 'Bull flag', right: 'Sharp rally, then a short tight drift' },
          { left: 'Ascending triangle', right: 'Flat top with rising lows' },
          { left: 'Cup and handle', right: 'Rounded U base, small pullback, breakout' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The honest part: limits of TA', html: `<p>You now know a lot of chart reading. Time for the most important lesson in the chapter: what it <em>cannot</em> do.</p><ul><li><strong>Indicators are derived from price.</strong> Moving averages, RSI, MACD and Bollinger Bands are all math on the same closes (plus volume). Ten indicators are not ten independent witnesses. They are one witness telling the story ten ways.</li><li><strong>Nothing works always.</strong> Every pattern and indicator fails regularly. Even good setups lose often. The edge, if there is one, is small and only shows up over many trades.</li><li><strong>Hindsight makes patterns look perfect.</strong> Looking back, you see the head and shoulders that worked. You do not notice the dozens of almost-patterns that went nowhere.</li><li><strong>Edges fade.</strong> When a simple pattern becomes famous enough, lots of traders act on it, and whatever advantage it had tends to shrink.</li></ul><p>Academic research on technical analysis is mixed. There is fairly well-documented evidence for momentum effects in markets, while results for many classic chart patterns are inconsistent, especially after trading costs.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Charts are a map of where the crowd has been. Useful. But no map has ever stopped a cliff from being a cliff." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Confirmation bias: your brain draws patterns', html: `<p>Human brains are pattern-finding machines. We see faces in clouds and animals in constellations. Charts are a perfect playground for that.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="The tendency to notice and believe information that supports what you already think, and ignore what contradicts it.">Confirmation bias</span> is the habit of seeing what you already want to see. If Dev already wants to buy a stock, he will find a bull flag. If he wants to short it, the same chart becomes a head and shoulders. He is not analyzing; he is decorating a decision he already made.</p><p>Defenses that actually help:</p><ul><li>Write down the pattern, trigger level and invalidation level <strong>before</strong> trading.</li><li>Ask: "What would this chart look like if I were wrong?" and draw that too.</li><li>Use fixed, written rules (like Bolt does) so you cannot reshape the pattern after the fact.</li><li>Track results honestly in a journal, including the patterns you skipped.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If RSI, MACD and a moving-average crossover all give a buy signal at the same time, you have three independent confirmations.', answer: false, explain: 'All three are calculated from the same closing prices. Agreement among them is partly guaranteed by the shared input. Independent evidence would be things like volume, a fundamental catalyst, or a higher-timeframe level.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'TA without risk management is gambling with extra steps', html: `<p>Even skilled chart readers are wrong a lot. What keeps them in business is that their losses are small and planned. Every pattern gives you a natural invalidation level: below the handle, above the right shoulder, back inside the flag. That level is where a stop belongs. The next chapter covers exactly how to size trades so that being wrong is survivable.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Which approach best protects a trader from fooling themselves with chart patterns?', options: ['Adding more indicators until they all agree', 'Writing the trigger and invalidation levels down before entering, and following them', 'Only trading patterns that are visible on the 1-minute chart', 'Waiting until a pattern is obvious in hindsight'], answer: 1, explain: 'Pre-committing to written levels prevents reshaping the story after the fact, which is the core of confirmation bias.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Pattern pocket guide', cards: [
          { front: 'Neckline', back: 'Trigger line of a head and shoulders. A break completes the pattern.' },
          { front: 'Measured move', back: 'Pattern height projected from the breakout. A rough target, not a promise.' },
          { front: 'Continuation pattern', back: 'A pause inside a trend, like a flag or triangle, that may resolve in the trend direction.' },
          { front: 'Reversal pattern', back: 'A shape suggesting a trend may be ending, like a double top.' },
          { front: 'Invalidation level', back: 'The price that proves your pattern idea wrong. Your stop usually goes near it.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'When is a head and shoulders top considered complete?', options: ['When the right shoulder forms', 'When price breaks below the neckline', 'When the head reaches a new high', 'When RSI hits 70'], answer: 1, explain: 'The neckline break is the trigger. Until then it is only a possible pattern.' },
          { q: 'An ascending triangle has...', options: ['A flat bottom and falling highs', 'Two equal peaks', 'A flat top and rising lows', 'A rounded U-shaped base'], answer: 2, explain: 'Flat resistance on top, higher lows underneath: buyers keep stepping in higher.' },
          { q: 'A head and shoulders has its head at $90 and its neckline at $80. Roughly where does the measured move point after a neckline break?', options: ['$70', '$60', '$85', '$100'], answer: 0, explain: 'Height = $10. Project down from the $80 neckline: about $70. A rough guide only.' },
          { q: 'Why are RSI, MACD and moving averages not independent confirmations of each other?', options: ['They use different exchanges', 'They update on different days', 'They are owned by different companies', 'They are all calculated from the same price data'], answer: 3, explain: 'They are transformations of the same closes, so they naturally tend to agree.' },
          { q: 'Dev sees a bull flag on a stock he already wanted to buy, then sees a bear flag on a stock he already wanted to short. What is most likely going on?', options: ['Confirmation bias: he is finding patterns that fit his existing view', 'He has a reliable edge', 'Flags always work', 'The charting software is broken'], answer: 0, explain: 'When the pattern you spot always matches the trade you already wanted, your brain is decorating a decision, not analyzing.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'A stock in an uptrend breaks above $50 resistance on 3x normal volume, then pulls back to $50.20 on light volume and holds. Which combination of ideas best describes this?', options: ['Bearish engulfing and a selling climax', 'Breakout with volume confirmation, then a retest where old resistance acts as support', 'A death cross followed by divergence', 'A squeeze followed by a fakeout'], answer: 1, explain: 'Heavy volume confirms the breakout. The light-volume pullback to the old ceiling that holds is role reversal: resistance became support.' },
    { q: 'Closes for 5 days: $30, $31, $33, $32, $34. Price is now $34. Which statement is true?', options: ['The 5-day SMA is $33, so price is above it', 'The 5-day SMA is $34, so price equals it', 'The 5-day SMA is $32, so price is above it', 'The 5-day SMA is $31, so price is below it'], answer: 2, explain: '30 + 31 + 33 + 32 + 34 = 160, and 160 / 5 = $32. Price at $34 is above the average.' },
    { q: 'In a strong uptrend, RSI has been above 70 for weeks and the stock keeps walking its upper Bollinger Band. What is the most defensible conclusion?', options: ['Both indicators confirm a strong trend; neither is a sell signal by itself', 'Both indicators are flashing an urgent sell', 'The stock is oversold', 'The bands are in a squeeze'], answer: 0, explain: 'Overbought RSI and band-walking are both typical of powerful trends. Tops need price evidence, like a broken higher low.' },
    { q: 'MACD is above zero but the histogram has shrunk for five straight days, and price just made a higher high while MACD made a lower high. What does this suggest?', options: ['Momentum is accelerating', 'The 12 EMA is below the 26 EMA', 'A golden cross just happened', 'Upward momentum is fading (bearish divergence), though the trend has not broken yet'], answer: 3, explain: 'Shrinking positive histogram and a lower MACD high against a higher price high both point to waning momentum. Price confirmation is still needed.' },
    { q: 'Maya wants to day trade a stock that is far below VWAP on a strong down-trend day, betting it will snap back. What is the key risk?', options: ['VWAP only works on weekly charts', 'On trend days, price can keep running away from VWAP, so mean reversion fails', 'VWAP cannot be calculated before noon', 'Being below VWAP guarantees a bounce'], answer: 1, explain: 'VWAP mean reversion works best on range days. On strong trend days, price can stay stretched from VWAP all session.' },
  ],
};
